course: dictionary
lesson: 9680
title: Sudden: happening without warning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «The door swung open with a sudden bang, and everyone jumped.» Look at the words around sudden. A bang that makes you jump comes out of nowhere, so sudden must mean quick and without warning.
explain: Sudden is often used in stories for surprise, like a sudden storm. In science and safety, sudden changes can be warnings, such as a sudden drop in temperature or sudden flooding.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The door swung open without warning, and everyone jumped.» It still makes sense, so you have understood sudden correctly.
fact: Sudden is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in a sudden noise.
fact: «Sudden» means happening quickly and without warning.
fact: «All of a sudden» is a phrase that also means quickly and without warning.
fact: «Horses startle at sudden noise», so stay calm and keep your distance from farm animals.
fact: Using «suddenly» again and again makes it less surprising; one surprise works better than ten.
exercise: «The storm came with a sudden crash of thunder.» What does sudden mean here? | Very loud and long | Coming back again and again | Slow and gentle | *Happening quickly and without warning
exercise: «Horses startle at sudden noise.» Find and copy the one word that means happening without warning. | horses | *sudden | noise | startle
exercise: «A sudden change in the weather can be a warning.» Which example is a sudden change? | A plant growing over many months | *A sudden drop in temperature | Reading a long book slowly | A calm walk in the park
test: «All of a sudden, the lights went out.» What does all of a sudden mean? | Slowly over a week | *Quickly, without warning | Only at night | On purpose, after a careful plan
test: «Using suddenly again and again stops it being sudden.» Why might a writer avoid using suddenly too often? | Because it is a rude word | Because it means happy | It is too short to read | *Because one surprise works better than many
exam: Which phrase means quickly and without warning? | Sudden death in sport, where the next goal wins | A sudden change, which takes a long time | *All of a sudden | Sudden noise, which is always quiet
